; `.cbor` payloads carrying an inline non-empty container. Since the `.cbor` rule-body force-wrap
; these rules mint WRAPPER structs whose INNER is the structural restricted wrapper
; (`NonEmptyU64List` / `NonEmptyMapTextToU64`) — which only the inner-base minting walk emits.
; Regression pinned (by this fixture's committed snapshots + the wasm-crate compile in
; `feature_corpus_compiles`): when these rules were transparent aliases, the rust_structs minting
; walk never visited alias base types, so the wasm crate referenced the restricted wrapper without
; emitting it (E0425 with generation exit 0; surfaced by the recombination wasm sweep's
; `bytes .cbor [+ uint]` composition) — the descent through the rule body must still reach the
; inner base now that a wrapper struct sits above it.
;
; Kept OUT of cbor_in_bytes.cddl deliberately: the ruby `cddl` gem (0.12.14) cannot PARSE an
; inline composite type2 controller (`bytes .cbor [+ uint]` — exit 65, the gap in
; cddl-matrix/upstream-reports/ruby-cddl-inline-composite-control-arg.md), and a spec-parse failure poisons every
; rule in its fixture for the `ir_conformance_corpus` ruby sweep — isolating them here keeps
; cbor_in_bytes' rules ruby-judgeable. Since the force-wrap these rules DO mint standalone
; conformance cases (each has a wire API of its own now), which the gem then cannot judge, so
; both ride RUBY_EXPECTED_FAIL with this justification instead of being silently skipped.
wrapped_nonempty = bytes .cbor [+ uint]
wrapped_nonempty_map = bytes .cbor { + tstr => uint }
